                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>a = 11, b = 33</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>No, b's value is copied into a but they are not connected after that.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>a = 11, b = 30</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct!</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>a = 10, b = 33</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Try the visualizer to see the step-by-step evaluation.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
      </activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="input-math">
    <title>Input and Math</title>
    <idx>input</idx>

    <p>When you use the <c>input()</c> command to get input from a user, the value is always treated as a string, 
      even if the user types in a number. If you want to use that input in a mathematical expression, you need to 
      convert it to a number first using the <c>int()</c> command for integers or <c>float()</c> command for 
      decimal numbers. For example:</p>
      <program language="python">
        <code>
ageStr = input("How old are you? ")
age = int(ageStr)
# Now we can do math with the age variable since it is an int instead of a string
print("Next year you will be", age + 1, "years old.")
        </code>
     </program>

      <activity label="input-math">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try the code below to see that there is an error when you try to do math with a string. 
          Then, fix the code by converting the input to an int before doing the math.
        </p>
      </statement>
    <program language="python" interactive="activecode">
        <code>
        ageStr = input("How old are you? ")
        # To fix the error, convert the string ageStr to an int and use it in the formula below
        yearsUntil100 = 100 - ageStr
        print("You will be 100 in", yearsUntil100, "years.")
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("You will be 100",self.getOutput(),"It works!")

          myTests().main()
         </tests>
    </program>
    </activity>


    <activity label="calculate-hours">
      <statement>
        <p>Write a program that asks the user for the number of minutes they have spent in class and then calculate 
          the number of hours, remaining minutes, and seconds. Fill in the formulas for 1, 2, and 3 below.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode">
        <code>
        # 1. Ask the user for the number of minutes they have spent in class
        minutesStr = input("How many minutes have you spent in class? ")
        # convert the input to an int
        minutes = int(minutesStr)

        # 1. calculate the hours in minutes by using integer division // (there are 60 minutes in an hour)
        hours = 
        # 2. calculate the remaining minutes in minutes using the mod % operator and 60
        remainingMinutes = 
        # 3. calculate the number of seconds in minutes with multiplication (there are 60 seconds in a minute)
        seconds = 
        
        # print the results
        print("You have spent", hours, "hour(s) and", remainingMinutes, "minute(s), and a total of", seconds, "second(s) in class.")
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("minutes//60",self.getEditorText().replace(" ", ""),"Use minutes//60 to calculate hours")
              self.assertIn("minutes%60",self.getEditorText().replace(" ", ""),"Use minutes%60 to calculate remaining minutes")
              self.assertIn("minutes*60",self.getEditorText().replace(" ", ""),"Use minutes*60 to calculate seconds")

          myTests().main()
         </tests>
        </program>
    </activity>
    </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="random-numbers">
    <title>Random Numbers</title>
    <idx>random numbers</idx>
    <idx>random</idx>

    <note>
      <title>1.7.B.1</title>
      <p>Apps use random number generation to create variety and unpredictability, such as in games,
        simulations, or randomly generated prompts.</p>
    </note>

    <p>Games would be boring if the same thing happened each time you played the
      game. Random numbers can be used in games to generate different actions or outcomes
      each time the game is played. Try a game app on your phone or computer and try to identify where
      random numbers are used. If you roll dice or flip a coin or if the enemies in the game do not always act 
    the same way, then random numbers are likely being used.</p>

    <note>
      <title>1.7.B.2</title>
      <p>Random values can support variation in app behavior, such as shuffling a playlist,
        selecting a random item, or assigning outcomes like rolling a die.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.7.B.3</title>
      <p>Programmers can determine the set of possible values produced by a random command by
        identifying the endpoints and whether they are included. When the following code runs, a
        random integer from <c>a</c> to <c>b</c>, including <c>a</c> and <c>b</c>, is generated and
        returned. Each result is equally likely to occur. The possible results of <c>random(a, b)</c>
        are all integers from <c>a</c> through <c>b</c>. <c>random(a, b)</c></p>
    </note>

    <p>In Python, there is a library called <c>random</c> that can be imported and used to generate 
    random values. Use <c>random.randint(start, end)</c> to generate a random integer from <c>start</c> to 
    <c>end</c>, including both endpoints. For example:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
import random
# pick a random number from 0 to 10
r = random.randint(0, 10)
      </code>
    </program>
    
    
    <p>Try running the program below a few times to see that it generates different
    random numbers each time it runs.</p>
  <activity label="random-num-gen">
    <statement>
      <p>Try the code below to see how to generate random numbers in Python. 
        Run it a few times to see that it generates different random numbers each time.
      Then, generate another random number from -10 to 10.</p>
    </statement>
  <program xml:id="random-num-generator" interactive="activecode" language="python">
    <code>
import random
# pick a random number from 1 to 100 
r = random.randint(1, 100)  
print(r)
# print another random number from -10 to 10
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              count = self.getEditorText().count("randint")
              self.assertGreaterEqual(count, 2, "randint used twice")

          myTests().main()
         </tests>
  </program>
</activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.7.B.4</title>
      <p>When a program uses random number generation, the same code can produce different results
        each time it runs.</p>
    </note>

    <p>Computers can’t usually generate truly random numbers without measuring something that is
      random (like static noise on radio frequencies or lava lamps). If they don’t have access to something like
      that, they must rely on generating <term>pseudorandom</term> numbers that look
      random even though they are created using a mathematical formula. Python takes the system
      time as the seed or starting point (since it is always changing) and then uses a mathematical formula to
      generate the next random number each time we ask for one.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="coding-challenge-random-turtle-project">
    <title>Coding Challenge: Random Turtle Project</title>

    <p>
      Let's practice with turtles drawing with random colors and random movements. 
      Colors on computers and on the web can be set using RGB (red, green, blue) values that range from 0 to 255 (which can be saved in 8 bits). 
      Try the <url href="https://www.google.com/search?q=colorpicker">Google color picker</url> and look at the RGB values for different colors
      below the selected color.  Any color can be made by mixing different amounts of red, green, and blue light. Mixing light is 
      different than mixing paint, so when you mix red, green, and blue light, you get white light.
    </p>
    <image source="_static/Unit1images/RGB.png" width="40%" alt="Mixing red, green, and blue light to make colors"/>

    <activity label="rgb-color">
     <statement> <p>What RGB values do you need to make black? Try the <url href="https://www.google.com/search?q=colorpicker">Google color picker</url> to test.</p></statement>
     <choices  randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <p>0, 0, 0</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Correct! Black light is the default state of no light. For example at nighttime, all is black because of there is no sunlight.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>255, 255, 255</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>This is white, not black. If you mix all three primary RGB colors of light, you get white.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>255, 0, 0</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>This is red light, not black. Red light is made with all red and no green or blue.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>0, 255, 255</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>This is cyan light, not black. Cyan light is made with all green and blue and no red.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
     </choices>
    </activity>

    <activity label="rand-predict">
     <statement> <p>The assignment statement <c>x = random.randint(-200, 200)</c> can be used to generate a random x-coordinate for a turtle,
      because it is initially placed at coordinates (0, 0) and can move 200 pixels to the right to 200 or left to -200. 
      Which of the following numbers cannot be generated by this expression?</p></statement>
     <choices  randomize="yes">
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>0</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>0 is between -200 and 200, so it can be generated.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>-200</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>-200 is between -200 and 200, so it can be generated.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>200</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>200 is between -200 and 200, so it can be generated.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <p>-255</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>-255 is not between -200 and 200, so it cannot be generated by random.randint(-200, 200).</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
     </choices>
    </activity>

     <project label="coding-challenge-random-turtle">
      <title>Random Turtle Project</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the code below, you will use random numbers to set the background color, 
          pen color, and turtle coordinates. Right now it uses default colors.
          Change the lines of code that say "# Change these to use random.randint". 
          Run the code a few times to see that it generates different random colors and movements each time it runs. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    <program xml:id="random-turtle" interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
import turtle
import random
screen = turtle.Screen()
turt = turtle.Turtle()

# Choose random RGB values for a light background color from 100 to 255
# You can choose different ranges within that for r, g, and b.
r = 255    # Change these to use random.randint(start,end)
g = 255
b = 255
# Set the background color using the random RGB values (scaled by /255)
screen.bgcolor(r/255, g/255, b/255)  

# Choose random RGB values for a dark pen color from 0 to 100
# You can choose different ranges within that for r, g, and b.
r = 0    # Change these to use random.randint(start,end)
g = 0
b = 0
# Set the pen color using the random RGB values (scaled by / 255)
turt.color(r/255, g/255, b/255)  

# Choose a random x and y coordinate from -200 to 200
x = 100   # Change these to use random.randint(start,end)
y = 100
turt.goto(x, y)  # Move the turtle to the random (x, y) coordinates

# Choose a random angle from 0 to 360 and a random distance from 20 to 100
angle = 45   # Change these to use random.randint(start,end)
distance = 50
turt.right(angle)       # Turn the turtle right by the random angle
turt.forward(distance)  # Move the turtle forward by the random distance
turt.goto(0, 0) 

# Repeat the code above to choose random colors, 
# random x, y, angle, distance and move the turtle at least 3 times

      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
                count = self.getEditorText().count("randint")
                self.assertGreaterEqual(count, 30, "at least 30 randint used")

          myTests().main()
       </tests>
    </program>
    </project>

     <activity label="turtle-rand-reflection">
      <title>Project Reflection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Describe how you used random numbers in your turtle project. 
          What was the range of random numbers you used for the RGB values and the x, y coordinates? 
          How did the random numbers enhance the project?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response />

    </activity>
